# ConvX

ConvX is a fully convolutional explainable image-classification method. The network produces class scores and class-specific spatial heatmaps in one forward pass. ConvX+ adds optional pixel-level supervision from segmentation masks or sparse scribble annotations.

This repository is organized from the supplied working code and the accompanying paper. The model and training procedures are retained; files are grouped into a reusable package and runnable scripts. Dataset files, checkpoints, generated plots, and the original virtual environment are intentionally kept out of the repository.

## Paper

Alex George, Lyudmila Mihaylova, and Sean Anderson, “ConvX: Efficient XAI for Real-Time Computer Vision with Single Forward-Pass Explanations.” The supplied manuscript reports mIoU 0.35 for image-label-only ConvX, 0.48 for ConvX+ with five dense masks per class, and 0.45 with five scribble annotations per class. These are reported paper results, not a guarantee that every local run will reproduce them.

## Repository layout

```text
ConvX/
├── src/convx/          # Model, losses, datasets, metrics, and training engines
├── scripts/            # Training, evaluation, data, and visualization entry points
├── data_sbd/           # Local SBD dataset (not tracked by Git)
├── data_voc/           # Local PASCAL VOC dataset (not tracked by Git)
├── data_scribbles/     # Local scribble annotations (not tracked by Git)
├── models/             # Generated model checkpoints (not tracked by Git)
├── plots/              # Generated visualizations (not tracked by Git)
├── metrics/            # Generated metric reports (not tracked by Git)
├── requirements.txt
└── pyproject.toml
```

The small `supervised_indices_5_per_class.json` file is included so the five-per-class training subset can use the same saved sample indices as the supplied project.

## Setup in VS Code on Windows

Open this folder (`ConvX`) in VS Code, then run these commands in its integrated PowerShell terminal:

```powershell
py -m venv .venv
.\.venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1
python -m pip install --upgrade pip
python -m pip install -r requirements.txt
python -m pip install -e .
```

If you need a particular CUDA-enabled PyTorch build, install the matching `torch` and `torchvision` packages for your machine using the official PyTorch selector before installing the remaining requirements. The first model run may also download torchvision's pretrained ResNet-50 weights.

### GPU / CUDA troubleshooting (Windows)

ConvX chooses its compute device automatically: `src/convx/config.py` sets `DEVICE` to `cuda` when `torch.cuda.is_available()` is true, and otherwise uses the CPU. This means no code change is normally needed to switch between CPU and an available NVIDIA GPU. However, automatic selection cannot add CUDA support to a CPU-only PyTorch installation. For example, `torch 2.14.0+cpu`, `torch.version.cuda == None`, and `torch.cuda.is_available() == False` indicate that the active environment has a CPU-only build; training will run on the CPU even if an NVIDIA GPU is installed.

First, activate the project's virtual environment in the VS Code terminal and check which Python and PyTorch that terminal is using:

```powershell
python -c "import sys, torch; print('Python:', sys.executable); print('PyTorch:', torch.__version__); print('CUDA build:', torch.version.cuda); print('CUDA available:', torch.cuda.is_available()); print('GPU:', torch.cuda.get_device_name(0) if torch.cuda.is_available() else 'not detected')"
```

Make sure `Python:` points to this repository's `.venv\Scripts\python.exe`. If `CUDA available` is `False` and `PyTorch` ends in `+cpu`, use the [official PyTorch installation selector](https://pytorch.org/get-started/locally/) to choose Windows, Pip, Python, and a CUDA compute platform supported by your NVIDIA driver. Run the generated installation command in the activated `.venv`, then rerun the check above. Also check that `nvidia-smi` detects the GPU and that VS Code is using the same `.venv` interpreter. The exact install command depends on the PyTorch release and supported driver, so use the selector rather than copying a stale version-specific command.

The project's device choice is printed at the start of its training scripts as `Target Compute Unit`. If it says `cpu`, PyTorch does not currently expose CUDA in the interpreter running the script. Installing a CUDA-enabled PyTorch build is a separate environment setup step; `requirements.txt` intentionally does not force a CUDA wheel because the correct build depends on the user's system.

The included VS Code launch configurations set the working directory to the repository root. When running commands in a terminal, run them from this root folder because dataset and output paths in the existing scripts are relative to it.

## Data

Before training or generating scribbles, run the dataset preparation script from the repository root:

```powershell
python -m scripts.prepare_data
```

It downloads and validates the SBD `train_noval` segmentation split in `data_sbd` and the PASCAL VOC 2012 validation split in `data_voc`. Torchvision skips a dataset when it is already present and valid. The first run needs internet access; the dataset files are not included in this repository. Use `python -m scripts.prepare_data --sbd-only` when you only need SBD for the scribble annotator, or `--voc-only` to prepare only VOC. See the [Torchvision dataset documentation](https://docs.pytorch.org/vision/stable/datasets.html) for details.

For scribble-supervised runs, put the generated `.npy` annotations in `data_scribbles` using the `scribble_<dataset-index>.npy` naming convention. Run the annotator from the repository root with:

```powershell
python -m scripts.generate_scribbles
```

## Common commands

Run these from the repository root after setup. Prepare the datasets first with `python -m scripts.prepare_data`.

```powershell
# Train/evaluate the image-label-only ConvX model
python -m scripts.train_convx

# Train the standard classifier baseline
python -m scripts.train_classifier

# Train ConvX+ with dense masks or sparse scribbles
python -m scripts.train_convx_plus_mask
python -m scripts.train_convx_plus_scribble

# Run data pipeline diagnostics
python -m scripts.verify_pipeline
python -m scripts.verify_scribble_dataset

# Run paper evaluation and visualization workflows
python -m scripts.run_explainability_metrics
python -m scripts.run_timing
python -m scripts.visual_comparison
python -m scripts.other_plots
```

Training and evaluation scripts write checkpoints beneath `models/` and generated artifacts beneath `plots/` or `metrics/`. These paths are ignored by Git. Some evaluation scripts require checkpoints from the corresponding training runs.

